response:
To implement bubble sort in C++ for sorting an integer array in ascending order with minimal memory usage and error prevention, you can follow these steps:

1. Define a function `bubbleSort(int arr[], int n)` that takes the unsorted array and its size as arguments.

2. Initialize two variables `i`, `j` and a flag `swapped` to keep track of the iteration and whether any swaps were made in a pass.

3. Loop through all the elements from the beginning to the end (n-1), comparing adjacent elements and swapping them if they are in the wrong order. Increment the `i` variable to move forward by one position.

4. If no swaps were made in this pass, then the array is already sorted, and we can break out of the outer loop.

5. Decrement the number of iterations remaining by one in each pass, until all elements are sorted.

Here's the code implementation:

```cpp
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    bool swapped = true;
    int i, j;
    while (swapped) {
        swapped = false;
        for (i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            if (arr[i] > arr[i+1]) {
                swap(arr[i], arr[i+1]);
                swapped = true;
            }
        }
        n--;
    }
}
```

