To fix the CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) error in Wordpress when using Next.js, you can follow these steps:

1. Install the `cors` middleware package in your Next.js project:

```bash
npm install --save cors
```

2. Add the `cors` middleware to your `server/index.js` file:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const next = require('next');
const app = next({ dev: process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production' });
const handle = app.getRequestHandler();
const cors = require('cors');

app.prepare()
  .then(() => {
    const server = express();

    // Enable CORS
    server.use(cors());

    server.all('*', (req, res) => {
      return handle(req, res);
    });

    const port = process.env.PORT || 3000;
    server.listen(port, (err) => {
      if (err) throw err;
      console.log(`Next.js server ready on http://localhost:${port}`);
    });
  })
  .catch((ex) => {
    console.error(ex.stack);
    process.exit(1);
  });
```

In this example, we first import the `cors` module and then enable it by calling the `cors()` function inside our Express application. This middleware allows us to specify which origins, methods, headers, and exposed headers are allowed to access our API.

By default, `cors()` allows all origins (`*`) and all HTTP methods (`'*'`). However, you may want to restrict access to specific origins or methods. In that case, you can customize the options passed to `cors()`. For more information about the available options, please refer to the official documentation.

After enabling CORS, we add an all-encompassing route that passes any request to our Next.js app using `handle()`. This ensures that our Next.js app handles all requests, even those with preflight requests.

